  [1mNext fork — how [38;5;153mapp.js[39m gets at the settings.[0m This one decides whether [38;5;153mindex.html[39m changes and whether the webapp
  becomes ES-module-based:

  - [1mGlobal script[0m — add [38;5;153m<script src="settings.js">[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m; settings.js sets [38;5;153mwindow.APP_SETTINGS[39m. Buys: matches
    exactly how the page works today, nothing else changes. Costs: one more global, and load order in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes
    load-bearing. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m does [38;5;153mexport const settings[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m does [38;5;153mimport[39m, and [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script [39m
    [38;5;153mtype="module" src="app.js">[39m. Buys: explicit dependency, no globals, the direction you'd want if this grows. Costs:
    [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m is deferred and CORS-restricted, so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m via [38;5;153mfile://[39m stops working — you'd need a local
    server to test. That restriction is the part that's annoying to discover later.

  I recommend [1mglobal script[0m: it's a two-line change that preserves how you currently run the page, and the module's
  interface ([38;5;153mAPP_SETTINGS.apiBaseUrl[39m) is identical under either, so moving to ES modules later is a mechanical edit
  rather than a redesign.
